From b20a148002cb66e423979f3d1ae64b450c19fe31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 11:31:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08905/11362] exp6258: the goal veto admits 21 of 26 useless
 predicates; my false-reject claim was wrong

Puts a number on the previous entry's code-path analysis using only data already
on disk -- exp6256's 8 freshly induced predicates plus exp6257's 21 measurable
stored ones, 29 with both axes. No GPU: re-inducing would have regenerated numbers
that already existed.

At the live threshold of 1.0: 21 FALSE ACCEPTS, 5 true accepts, 3 true rejects,
and 0 false rejects. Acceptance precision 0.1923 -- of the 26 predicates the veto
admits, only 5 fire on a real win, and each becomes plan_in_model's termination
condition.

CORRECTION TO WHAT I WROTE BEFORE MEASURING. That entry claimed the inversion had
two arms: the veto admits constant-False predicates AND rejects discriminating
ones that fire once too often. The first arm is strongly supported, 21 of 29. The
second has ZERO support -- there were no false rejects at all. Every discriminating
predicate in this population also had perfect specificity, so the veto never had
to choose between them. That arm is arithmetically possible, was not observed, and
should not be repeated as a finding.

The defect is therefore ONE-SIDED: the veto over-admits, it does not over-reject.
That changes the fix. Lowering the threshold would make it strictly worse, since
it only ever admits more. What is needed is an ADDITIONAL condition, not a relaxed
one.

Fresh inductions are worse than stored: 7 of 8 freshly induced predicates are
false accepts with 0 true accepts, against 14 of 21 and 5 true accepts for stored.

Does not observe the live agent -- it shows what the live threshold would do to
predicates of the kind this project produces, on public games with
development-proxy win grids, not hidden-game level 1. n=29.

The live threshold is still deliberately unchanged. Wiring the veto to also
require sensitivity would have rejected 21 of the 26 it admits, but at level 1 no
win grid exists, so the honest options there are narrower and it remains an
operator decision.

